body:
## Problem

GetOpenPullRequestsAsync fetches all open PRs, then issues one additional GraphQL request per PR, sequentially awaited one at a time, to fetch status-check details.

## Evidence

PullRequestReportService.cs lines 44-51:
var rawPullRequests = await _gitHub.GetOpenPullRequestsAsync(owner, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
var enrichedPullRequests = new List<GitHubPullRequest>(rawPullRequests.Count);
foreach (var pullRequest in rawPullRequests)
{
    var statusDetails = await _gitHub.GetPullRequestStatusDetailsAsync(pullRequest, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
    enrichedPullRequests.Add(pullRequest with { StatusDetails = statusDetails });
}

## Impact

With the configured defaults (RepositoryLimit=1000, PullRequestLimitPerRepository=100 per appsettings.json/README), an owner with 100-200 open PRs across their repos triggers 100-200 sequential GraphQL round-trips with no concurrency, on top of repository-listing pagination calls. At realistic GraphQL latency (150-300ms/call) that adds 15-60+ seconds to a single request -- a likely timeout for any reverse proxy/load balancer, and a fast way to exhaust GitHub's point-based rate limit on one request.

## Fix

Fetch status-check data with bounded concurrency (Task.WhenAll behind a SemaphoreSlim, or Parallel.ForEachAsync), or better: extend OpenPullRequestsQuery to request statusCheckRollup/branchProtectionRule inline on each PR node so status data returns in the same query as the PR list, eliminating the second round-trip entirely.

## Resolution
Replaced the sequential foreach with Parallel.ForEachAsync (MaxDegreeOfParallelism=8) in PullRequestReportService.FetchAndCacheAsync, fetching status details concurrently instead of one round-trip at a time.

## Problem

/health/ready -- used by the documented Coolify deployment as the orchestrator's health path -- reports "ready" purely based on whether a non-empty token string is configured. It never calls GitHub, so it cannot detect a revoked/expired token or a GitHub outage.

## Evidence

Program.cs lines 67-77:
static IResult GetReadiness(IOptionsMonitor<GitHubOptions> options)
{
    if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(options.CurrentValue.Token))
    {
        return Results.Ok(new HealthResponse("ready"));
    }
    return Results.Problem(title: "GitHub token is not configured.", statusCode: StatusCodes.Status503ServiceUnavailable);
}
README.md lines 252-261 (Coolify deployment section) explicitly recommends /health/ready as the orchestrator's Health path.

## Impact

If the configured token is revoked, expired, or GitHub is unreachable, every real request to /api/github/open-pull-requests fails with a 503, while /health/ready keeps reporting 200/"ready" throughout since it never checks reachability. An orchestrator relying on this probe for restart/alerting decisions will never detect the degraded state.

## Fix

Have the readiness check reflect the last known GitHub call outcome (a cached success/failure flag updated by GitHubGraphQlClient), or perform a cheap, short-timeout authenticated GitHub call (e.g. the rateLimit GraphQL field) on a short cache, so a revoked token or GitHub outage flips readiness to unavailable.

## Resolution
Added GitHubApiHealthState singleton, updated by GitHubGraphQlClient on every success/failure; /health/ready now also checks IsHealthy, not just token presence.

## Problem

On any non-success HTTP status from the GitHub GraphQL endpoint, the entire response body is read and logged at Warning level with no length cap or truncation.

## Evidence

GitHubGraphQlClient.cs lines 254-259:
if (!response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
{
    var body = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
    _logger.LogWarning("GitHub GraphQL query failed with HTTP {StatusCode}: {Body}", (int)response.StatusCode, body);
    throw new GitHubQueryException($"GitHub GraphQL query failed with HTTP {(int)response.StatusCode}.");
}

## Impact

GitHub API failures don't always return small JSON error bodies -- an edge/CDN-level outage or misroute can return a large HTML error page instead. Logging that verbatim, with no cap, at Warning level on every failed call (which per the N+1/refresh findings can happen frequently) risks noisy, oversized log entries and unnecessary log-ingestion cost.

## Fix

Truncate the logged body to a bounded length (e.g. the first 500 characters) before including it in the log message.

## Resolution
Added a Truncate() helper capping the logged GitHub error response body at 500 characters in GitHubGraphQlClient.ExecuteAsync.

## Problem

The `cache` table is created once, in the constructor. Every subsequent operation opens a fresh
connection with `SqliteOpenMode.ReadWriteCreate`, which silently **recreates** a missing
database file — empty, with no table. From that moment every read and write raises
`SQLITE_ERROR: no such table: cache`, is swallowed by the fail-open catch, and logs a warning.
The tier never recovers; only a process restart re-runs the DDL.

Fail-open means the service keeps serving, so nothing surfaces as an error. What surfaces is a
cache that is permanently cold plus one warning line per cache operation, forever.

## Evidence

Schema creation is constructor-only —
`src/GhPrApi/Caching/SqliteDistributedCache.cs:50-61`:

```csharp
using var connection = OpenConnection();
using var command = connection.CreateCommand();
command.CommandText = """
    PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL;
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cache ( ... );
    ...
    """;
command.ExecuteNonQuery();
```

The runtime path never re-checks — `:184-189`:

```csharp
private SqliteConnection OpenConnection()
{
    var connection = new SqliteConnection(_connectionString);
    connection.Open();
    return connection;
}
```

and the connection string pins `Mode = SqliteOpenMode.ReadWriteCreate` (`:47`), so a missing
file is created rather than reported.

Once the table is gone, `Get` (`:78`) and `Set` (`:116`) hit their `catch (SqliteException)`
and call `LogFailure`, which the class comment at `:176-177` already flags as unthrottled:
"one line per failed operation. A hard-down database with the rate limiter wide open is noisy".

## Impact

Failing scenario: an operator clears a stale cache the obvious way — `rm /data/cache.db` — or a
Docker volume is detached and reattached empty. The process keeps running and keeps answering
requests, so nothing looks broken. But every listing and every per-PR status now misses L2, so
each restart-surviving optimisation the two-tier design exists for is gone, and each request
emits one warning per cache operation (dozens per report). GitHub quota consumption rises to
the uncached rate silently.

## Fix

Recreate the schema when the table has gone missing, once, then retry. Keeping it inside the
existing failure path costs nothing on the healthy path:

```csharp
private const int SqliteErrorGeneric = 1; // SQLITE_ERROR, raised by "no such table"

private void EnsureSchema(SqliteConnection connection)
{
    using var command = connection.CreateCommand();
    command.CommandText = SchemaSql; // the DDL string, hoisted out of the constructor
    command.ExecuteNonQuery();
}
```

then in each catch, before degrading:

```csharp
catch (SqliteException ex) when (ex.SqliteErrorCode == SqliteErrorGeneric && ex.Message.Contains("no such table", StringComparison.Ordinal))
{
    // The file was deleted or replaced under us; ReadWriteCreate silently made an empty one.
    // Re-run the DDL so the tier recovers instead of warning once per operation until restart.
    ...
}
```

Add a test that deletes `_path` between a `Set` and the next `Set`/`Get` and asserts the round
trip works again.

## Resolution
Execute() detects 'no such table' and re-runs the DDL once before retrying. Covered by A_deleted_database_file_is_recreated_rather_than_killing_the_tier.

## Problem

The test asserts only that *some* exception is thrown. The production code it protects does not
catch some exception — it catches three specific types. A regression that changes the thrown
type to a fourth would leave this test green and take the whole service down at startup.

## Evidence

The test — `tests/GhPrApi.Tests/SqliteDistributedCacheTests.cs:127-133`:

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Constructor_throws_when_the_path_is_not_writable()
{
    var unwritable = Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), $"ghpr-{Guid.NewGuid():N}", "nested", "cache.db");

    Assert.ThrowsAny<Exception>(() => new SqliteDistributedCache(unwritable, new FakeTimeProvider()));
}
```

The code it exists for — `src/GhPrApi/Program.cs:62-78`:

```csharp
try
{
    return new SqliteDistributedCache(...);
}
catch (Exception ex) when (ex is SqliteException or IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException)
{
    // Fail open. ...
    return new MemoryDistributedCache(...);
}
```

`ThrowsAny<Exception>` and that filter do not agree. The test's own value is the bridge between
them, and it asserts nothing about the type.

## Impact

Failing scenario: a `Microsoft.Data.Sqlite` upgrade changes an unopenable path from
`SqliteException` to `InvalidOperationException` (a plausible change — the library already uses
`InvalidOperationException` for connection-state errors). This test still passes: an
`InvalidOperationException` is an `Exception`. `Program.cs`'s `when` filter does not match, the
exception escapes the DI factory, and `builder.Build()` throws — the process fails to start.

The fail-open guarantee that `EndpointTests.App_starts_and_serves_when_the_cache_path_is_unusable`
(`:193-204`) exists to protect is broken, and only that second test catches it. This one, the
unit-level test written specifically about the throw, sails through.

## Fix

Assert the type is one the production filter handles, and say why:

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Constructor_throws_a_type_the_startup_fallback_catches()
{
    var unwritable = Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), $"ghpr-{Guid.NewGuid():N}", "nested", "cache.db");

    // Program.cs falls back to the in-memory tier on SqliteException/IOException/
    // UnauthorizedAccessException only. Anything else escapes the DI factory and stops the
    // process from starting, so the type is the contract -- not merely that it throws.
    var exception = Assert.ThrowsAny<Exception>(
        () => new SqliteDistributedCache(unwritable, new FakeTimeProvider()));

    Assert.True(
        exception is SqliteException or IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException,
        $"Program.cs's fallback filter does not catch {exception.GetType().Name}.");
}
```

## Resolution
Renamed to Constructor_throws_a_type_the_startup_fallback_catches and now asserts the exception is one Program.cs's filter handles.

## Problem

`GetToken()` throws `GitHubQueryException("GitHub token is not configured.")`. The endpoint's
catch discards the exception object entirely and answers with a fixed message that names the
wrong culprit: `Unable to query GitHub.` GitHub was never contacted. Nothing is logged on this
path either, so the accurate message exists only inside an exception nobody reads.

## Evidence

Thrown without logging — `src/GhPrApi/GitHub/GitHubGraphQlClient.cs:327-336`:

```csharp
private string GetToken()
{
    var token = _options.CurrentValue.Token;
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(token))
    {
        throw new GitHubQueryException("GitHub token is not configured.");
    }

    return token;
}
```

Caught and discarded — `src/GhPrApi/Program.cs:271-279`:

```csharp
catch (GitHubQueryException)
{
    return format switch
    {
        ResponseFormat.Markdown => Results.Text("Unable to query GitHub.", ...),
        ResponseFormat.Html => Results.Text(HtmlReportFormatter.FormatError("Unable to query GitHub."), ...),
        _ => Results.Problem(title: "Unable to query GitHub.", ...),
    };
}
```

The exception is not bound (`catch (GitHubQueryException)`, no identifier), so its message
cannot reach a log or a response. The other `GitHubQueryException` sites do log first
(`GitHubGraphQlClient.cs:288`, `:309`), which is why this gap is easy to miss — the endpoint's
silence is invisible until you hit the one path that throws without logging.

This is reachable by design: `Token` is deliberately not start-up validated. `Program.cs:35-41`
validates `Owner`, `CacheTtlSeconds`, `RepositoryLimit`, `PullRequestLimitPerRepository`,
`StatusCheckLimitPerPullRequest`, `CachePath` and `StatusCacheTtlSeconds` — every option except
the token — so the process starts without one and reports unready
(`GetReadiness`, `:161-168`).

## Impact

Failing scenario: an operator deploys without `GitHub__Token` (an unset secret, a typo'd
variable name). `/health/ready` correctly says `503 GitHub token is not configured.` But the
endpoint people actually call answers `503 Unable to query GitHub.` — which reads as "GitHub is
down", the one conclusion that sends the operator to look at GitHub's status page instead of
their own configuration. The logs contain nothing at all for that request, because the only
component that logs GitHub failures never ran.

## Fix

Bind the exception and log it, so the accurate cause reaches the operator without changing the
response contract README.md documents:

```csharp
catch (GitHubQueryException ex)
{
    // Bound and logged: the response message is a fixed public string, so this is the only
    // place the actual cause (a missing token, an upstream error) can reach an operator.
    logger.LogWarning(ex, "Report request failed: {Reason}", ex.Message);

    return format switch { ... };   // unchanged
}
```

`RenderReportAsync` takes an `ILogger<Program>` parameter — minimal-API handlers resolve it
from DI like the existing arguments. Add an endpoint test that configures an empty token and
asserts the report endpoint returns 503, so the path is at least covered.

## Resolution
The endpoint now binds and logs the GitHubQueryException at Warning before returning the fixed public message.

## Problem

Every `CreateFactory` call mints a unique SQLite cache path under the system temp directory.
The cache file is created as soon as a request touches the L2 tier, and nothing ever deletes
it. `EndpointTests` implements no cleanup at all.

`SqliteDistributedCacheTests` gets this right — it implements `IDisposable` and deletes its
file. `EndpointTests`, which creates an order of magnitude more of them, does not.

## Evidence

Path minted per factory — `tests/GhPrApi.Tests/EndpointTests.cs:288-289`:

```csharp
["GitHub:CachePath"] = cachePath
    ?? Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), $"ghpr-test-{Guid.NewGuid():N}.db"),
```

No cleanup exists — `EndpointTests` is declared `public sealed class EndpointTests` (`:18`)
with no `IDisposable`, no `IAsyncLifetime`, and no `Dispose` member anywhere in the file.

Compare `tests/GhPrApi.Tests/SqliteDistributedCacheTests.cs:152-159`, which does clean up:

```csharp
public void Dispose()
{
    SqliteConnection.ClearAllPools();
    if (File.Exists(_path))
    {
        File.Delete(_path);
    }
}
```

Measured, running only this class into an isolated temp directory:

```text
$ D=$(mktemp -d); TMPDIR=$D dotnet test --filter 'FullyQualifiedName~EndpointTests'
Passed!  - Failed: 0, Passed: 28, Skipped: 0, Total: 28

$ find "$D" -maxdepth 1 -type f | wc -l
22
$ du -sb "$D"
360448
```

22 files, ~360 KB, left behind by one filtered run of one test class.

## Impact

Failing scenario: a developer runs `dotnet test` in a watch loop or a pre-commit hook. Each
iteration deposits 22 more files. On a long-lived machine the temp directory accumulates
thousands of `ghpr-test-*.db` files; on a CI runner with a small tmpfs `/tmp`, repeated runs in
one job can exhaust it, and the failure surfaces as an unrelated SQLite "unable to open database
file" in whichever test happens to run next — a confusing, order-dependent red build.

The suite already knows how to clean up after itself; this class simply does not.

## Fix

Track the minted paths and delete them, mirroring the sibling class:

```csharp
public sealed class EndpointTests : IDisposable
{
    private readonly List<string> _cachePaths = [];

    ...

    private WebApplicationFactory<Program> CreateFactory(...)
    {
        var path = cachePath ?? Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), $"ghpr-test-{Guid.NewGuid():N}.db");
        _cachePaths.Add(path);
        ...
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        // Each factory mints its own SQLite file; without this a single run leaves 22 behind.
        SqliteConnection.ClearAllPools();
        foreach (var path in _cachePaths)
        {
            foreach (var file in new[] { path, path + "-wal", path + "-shm" })
            {
                if (File.Exists(file))
                {
                    File.Delete(file);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

`CreateFactory` has to stop being `static` for this; that is the only call-site change.

## Resolution
EndpointTests implements IDisposable and deletes each minted db plus its -wal/-shm sidecars. Verified with an isolated TMPDIR: 22 leftover files before, 0 after.

## Problem

The durable cache is sold on one property: cached work survives a restart. Proving that needs a
value to go out through HybridCache's serializer into SQLite and come back as an equal object.
No test does this. The two halves are each covered in isolation and the seam between them is
not covered at all.

## Evidence

`AddHybridCache()` appears exactly once in the test suite —
`tests/GhPrApi.Tests/PullRequestReportServiceTests.cs:200-202`:

```csharp
var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddHybridCache();
var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
```

No `IDistributedCache` is registered in that collection, so HybridCache runs **L1 only**. Every
caching assertion in that file — stampede coalescing, TTL reuse, refresh bypass, the
partial-failure retry — is measured against the in-memory tier with no serialization involved.

`SqliteDistributedCacheTests` covers the other half, but only at the byte level. Its
round-trip test (`:64-74`) writes `[7]` and reads `[7]`; every value in that file is a small
`byte[]` literal. It never sees a domain type.

`EndpointTests` wires the real `SqliteDistributedCache` via `Program.cs`, so L2 *writes* happen
incidentally. But no test evicts L1 and forces a read back out of L2 — within one process every
subsequent hit is served from memory. So the deserialize direction is never executed.

The types that would have to survive that trip are not trivial:
`GitHubOpenPullRequestsResult` -> `IReadOnlyList<GitHubPullRequest>` -> `Uri`,
`DateTimeOffset`, `GitHubActor?`, `IReadOnlyList<string>`, and a nested
`GitHubPullRequestStatusDetails?`. `GitHubModels.cs:32-34` records that this shape is already
constrained by the serializer:

```csharp
// RequiredStatusCheckNames is a list, not a set: HybridCache's L2 serializes every cached
// value and System.Text.Json cannot deserialize IReadOnlySet<T>.
```

That constraint is enforced by a comment. Nothing fails if someone breaks it.

## Impact

Failing scenario: a future change makes `GitHubPullRequest.Labels` an `IReadOnlySet<string>` —
a natural move, since the labels are already deduplicated
(`GitHubGraphQlClient.cs:364`). The full suite stays green: `PullRequestReportServiceTests`
never serializes, and `EndpointTests` only ever writes.

In production the L2 write succeeds and every read after a restart throws
`NotSupportedException` out of `System.Text.Json`. HybridCache surfaces backend exceptions to
the caller, so the first request after every redeploy returns HTTP 500 — until the entry
expires. The comment that warned about exactly this was three lines away from the change and
had no teeth.

## Fix

Add one test that closes the loop, using the real `SqliteDistributedCache` as L2 and a fresh
HybridCache to stand in for a restarted process:

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task A_cached_listing_survives_a_process_restart()
{
    // L2 is the only reason the SQLite tier exists, and its serializer constrains the shape of
    // every cached model (see GitHubModels.cs). A second provider over the same file is what a
    // redeploy looks like: L1 empty, L2 warm.
    var path = Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), $"ghpr-l2-{Guid.NewGuid():N}.db");
    try
    {
        var gitHub = new FakeGitHubGraphQlClient
        {
            ListingFactory = (_, _) => Task.FromResult(
                new GitHubOpenPullRequestsResult([TestPullRequests.Create(number: 1)], false)),
        };

        await CreateService(gitHub, cachePath: path).GetOpenPullRequestsAsync(null, false, CancellationToken.None);
        var afterRestart = await CreateService(gitHub, cachePath: path).GetOpenPullRequestsAsync(null, false, CancellationToken.None);

        Assert.Equal(1, gitHub.ListingCallCount);   // served from L2, not re-fetched
        Assert.Equal(1, afterRestart.TotalCount);
    }
    finally
    {
        SqliteConnection.ClearAllPools();
        File.Delete(path);
    }
}
```

`CreateService` needs an optional `cachePath` that registers
`services.AddSingleton<IDistributedCache>(new SqliteDistributedCache(cachePath, TimeProvider.System))`
before `AddHybridCache()`.

## Resolution
Added A_cached_listing_survives_a_process_restart, which drives a real SqliteDistributedCache L2 across two providers. Sequenced on the durable row actually landing, since HybridCache writes L2 on a background task; stable across three consecutive full runs.

## Problem

The rate limiter rejects with a bare `429` and no `Retry-After` header. A client has no way to
learn when the window reopens, so the only available strategy is to retry blindly — against a
limiter that is process-global, where blind retries from one caller consume the permits of
every other caller.

## Evidence

`src/GhPrApi/Program.cs:104-113`:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(options =>
{
    options.RejectionStatusCode = StatusCodes.Status429TooManyRequests;
    options.AddFixedWindowLimiter(RateLimiterPolicies.GitHubApi, limiterOptions =>
    {
        limiterOptions.PermitLimit = 10;
        limiterOptions.Window = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1);
        limiterOptions.QueueLimit = 0;
    });
});
```

`RejectionStatusCode` sets the status and nothing else. ASP.NET's rate limiter emits
`Retry-After` only from an explicit `OnRejected` callback; there is none here, so the header is
absent.

`EndpointTests.Eleventh_request_inside_one_window_is_rate_limited` (`:246-261`) asserts the 429
and does not look at headers, so the gap is not visible from the suite either.

The sharing property that makes this matter is documented and deliberate — README.md:83:

> The limit is **process-global and shared by all callers**, not per client — it exists to cap
> total load on the single upstream GitHub token, so one busy caller can exhaust the window for
> everyone.

`FixedWindowRateLimiter` already computes the value: its `RateLimitLease` exposes
`MetadataName.RetryAfter`. The information exists and is discarded.

## Impact

Failing scenario: a polling dashboard hits its 11th request and gets a 429 with no guidance. Its
retry policy — the sensible default in most HTTP clients — backs off a second and tries again.
It burns roughly 50 more rejected requests before the minute is out. Each of those consumes a
connection and a rate-limiter lease acquisition, and, because the limiter is shared, keeps the
window contended for every other caller, who are themselves also retrying blind.

A single header turns that into one wait and one request.

## Fix

Surface the value the limiter already produces:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(options =>
{
    options.RejectionStatusCode = StatusCodes.Status429TooManyRequests;

    // The limiter is process-global, so a client retrying blind spends everyone else's permits.
    // FixedWindowRateLimiter already knows when the window reopens; pass it on.
    options.OnRejected = (context, _) =>
    {
        if (context.Lease.TryGetMetadata(MetadataName.RetryAfter, out var retryAfter))
        {
            context.HttpContext.Response.Headers.RetryAfter =
                ((int)retryAfter.TotalSeconds).ToString(NumberFormatInfo.InvariantInfo);
        }

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    };

    options.AddFixedWindowLimiter(...);   // unchanged
});
```

`MetadataName` lives in `System.Threading.RateLimiting`. Extend
`Eleventh_request_inside_one_window_is_rate_limited` to assert
`rejected.Headers.RetryAfter is not null`, and document the header in README.md:83.

## Resolution
OnRejected forwards the limiter's RetryAfter metadata as a Retry-After header; asserted in Eleventh_request_inside_one_window_is_rate_limited and documented in README.
